            en: 'Braised Pork Belly is one of the most famous traditional Chinese dishes with a long history. The dish features a bright red color, is rich but not greasy, and melts in your mouth, symbolizing reunion and abundance. In traditional Chinese culture, Braised Pork Belly is often prepared during important festivals and family gatherings, symbolizing the taste of "home." The famous "Dongpo Pork" is a variation of this dish, said to have been created by the Song Dynasty poet Su Dongpo. The cooking technique of Braised Pork embodies the importance of "huohou" (heat control) in Chinese cooking, perfectly reflecting Chinese culinary philosophy.\n\n**Historical Background**: The history of Braised Pork Belly can be traced back to the Tang Dynasty, when it was called "red braised meat." The Song Dynasty literati Su Dongpo played an important role in promoting Braised Pork Belly, so it is also known as "Dongpo Pork."\n\n**Cultural Significance**: Braised Pork Belly symbolizes abundance and reunion in Chinese culture, often appearing in important festivals and family gatherings. Its cooking process embodies the importance of "heat control" in Chinese cooking, reflecting Chinese culinary philosophy.\n\n**Social Scenarios**: Suitable for family reunions, holiday celebrations and other important occasions. At New Years Eve dinner, birthday banquets and other occasions, Braised Pork Belly often appears as the main dish.'
