        culture: {
            zh: '麻婆豆腐是一道非常有代表性的川菜。它由嫩豆腐和肉末一起烹制而成，配上辣椒、花椒和豆瓣酱，颜色红亮，味道麻辣浓郁。豆腐柔软细嫩，而酱汁香辣鲜香，是许多人接触川菜时最熟悉的一道菜。\n\n这道菜的故事通常从19世纪的成都说起。相传在清朝同治年间，成都北郊万福桥附近有一家小饭铺，名叫"陈兴盛饭铺"。店主是一位姓陈的妇人，因为脸上有些麻点，邻里便称她为"陈麻婆"。当时来店里吃饭的客人多是挑油的工人，他们常带来一些豆腐和牛肉，请她帮忙简单烹调。为了让这些辛苦劳作的人吃得热乎又有味道，她把油烧热后加入辣椒和牛肉末炒香，再放入豆腐一起煮，最后撒上一些花椒。做出来的豆腐麻辣鲜香，很快在附近的人群中传开。人们渐渐把这道菜叫作"陈麻婆豆腐"，后来简化成"麻婆豆腐"。\n\n随着时间发展，这道菜的做法也不断变化。到了20世纪初，一些厨师在原有基础上加入豆豉等调料，让味道更加浓郁。虽然在某些历史时期它曾被改名为"麻辣豆腐"，但"麻婆豆腐"这个名字最终保留下来，并成为川菜中最具代表性的菜品之一。如今，这道菜不仅在中国各地餐馆常见，也在世界许多地方流行。例如在日本，厨师陈建民曾对它进行改良，使其成为非常受欢迎的中华料理之一。\n\n在文化上，麻婆豆腐常被用来代表川菜的味道特点。川菜讲究多层次的调味，而麻婆豆腐通常被总结为几种关键感受：麻、辣、香、嫩和鲜。花椒带来的微麻感与辣椒的辣味结合，再加上肉末和豆瓣酱的香气，让整道菜味道非常浓郁。同时，这道菜的名字也提醒人们，它最初来自普通人的厨房，而不是宫廷或贵族餐桌，这种从民间诞生的背景也是中国饮食文化的重要特点。\n\n在日常生活中，麻婆豆腐是一道非常常见的家常菜。它通常配着米饭一起吃，也经常出现在餐馆的菜单上。在家庭聚餐或朋友一起吃饭时，这道菜往往是大家都会接受的一种选择。对很多第一次尝试川菜的人来说，麻婆豆腐也是了解中国麻辣风味的一道经典入口。',
            en: 'Mapo Tofu is a very representative dish of Sichuan cuisine. It is made by cooking tender tofu with ground meat, accompanied by chili peppers, Sichuan peppercorns, and fermented bean paste, resulting in a bright red color and rich spicy and numbing flavor. The tofu is soft and delicate, while the sauce is fragrant, spicy, and savory, making it the most familiar dish for many people when they first encounter Sichuan cuisine.\n\nThe story of this dish usually begins in 19th century Chengdu. Legend has it that during the Tongzhi period of the Qing Dynasty, there was a small eatery called "Chen Xingsheng Restaurant" near Wanfu Bridge in the northern suburbs of Chengdu. The owner was a woman surnamed Chen, who was called "Chen Mapo" by neighbors because she had some pockmarks on her face. At that time, most of the customers who came to eat were oil porters. They often brought some tofu and beef, asking her to help cook them simply. To let these hardworking people eat something hot and flavorful, she heated the oil, added chili peppers and ground beef to fry until fragrant, then added the tofu to cook together, and finally sprinkled some Sichuan peppercorns. The resulting tofu was spicy, numbing, fresh, and fragrant, quickly spreading among nearby people. People gradually called this dish "Chen Mapo Tofu," which was later simplified to "Mapo Tofu."\n\nAs time developed, the preparation of this dish also continued to evolve. By the early 20th century, some chefs added fermented black beans and other seasonings on the original basis, making the taste even richer. Although it was once renamed "Spicy Tofu" during certain historical periods, the name "Mapo Tofu" was eventually retained and became one of the most representative dishes of Sichuan cuisine. Today, this dish is not only common in restaurants throughout China but also popular in many places around the world. For example, in Japan, chef Chen Jianmin once improved it, making it one of the most popular Chinese dishes.\n\nCulturally, Mapo Tofu is often used to represent the flavor characteristics of Sichuan cuisine. Sichuan cuisine emphasizes multi-layered seasoning, and Mapo Tofu is usually summarized into several key sensations: numbing, spicy, fragrant, tender, and fresh. The slight numbing sensation brought by Sichuan peppercorns combined with the spiciness of chili peppers, plus the aroma of ground meat and fermented bean paste, makes the whole dish very rich in flavor. At the same time, the name of this dish also reminds people that it originally came from ordinary people\'s kitchens, not from palace or noble tables. This background of being born from the folk is also an important characteristic of Chinese food culture.\n\nIn daily life, Mapo Tofu is a very common home-style dish. It is usually eaten with rice and often appears on restaurant menus. When having family meals or eating with friends, this dish is often a choice that everyone will accept. For many people trying Sichuan cuisine for the first time, Mapo Tofu is also a classic entry point to understand Chinese spicy and numbing flavors.'
        }
